.TH GXWI-REGISTRY-EDITOR 1 "2026-10-06" "Peios"
.SH NAME
gxwi-registry-editor \- browse and change the registry on a GXWI desktop
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gxwi-registry-editor
.RB [ \-\-key
.IR path " |"
.BR \-\-layers ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Registry Editor shows the registry: its keys as a tree, from Machine, Users
and CurrentUser, and the values of the key picked, each with its name, its
type in words and its data. A path typed at the top goes straight to its key.
Picking a value shows it in full, with the layer its data came from; with no
value picked, the pane describes the key. What the registry manual says of a
key and its values is shown with them, and values the manual documents but
that are not set are listed greyed, with their defaults.
.PP
Values are edited in a form for their type, and added and deleted; keys are
made, and deleted with everything under them, all or nothing. Changes go to
the layer chosen in Writes go to, base unless another is chosen. Layers\&...
opens the layers in a window of their own, to make, enable, disable and
delete them and set their precedence. Permissions\&... opens a key's permissions, or a
security descriptor a value holds, in the permissions editor. Files exports
a key to a registry document and imports one, and backs a key up and
restores it, each through the file dialog.
.PP
It reads and writes the registry as the person, through the kernel's
registry calls, with no more authority than they have. Each key's own
permissions say what they may do with it: list it, read its values, change
them, make keys under it, delete it. What they may not do is shown greyed,
and the pane says why; a key they may not list or read says so in the tree.
A key under one they may not list can still be reached by typing its path.
Backing up needs SeBackupPrivilege and restoring SeRestorePrivilege, which
Administrators hold. A layer precedence above 0 needs SeTcbPrivilege, which
Administrators do not hold.
.PP
The window watches the keys it shows, so a change made elsewhere appears
within a moment; Refresh (F5) reads everything again. It needs a desktop: run
with no GXWI_DISPLAY, it says so and ends.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-key " path"
Open on the key at
.I path
rather than on Machine. Names are separated by \e or /, as in
.BR Machine\eSystem\eServices .
.TP
.B \-\-layers
Open only the layers window. The editor starts itself this way from
Layers\&...; there is no need to give it by hand.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/apps/dev.peios.gxwi-registry-editor.toml
Its application declaration, which lists it in the launcher.
.TP
.I /usr/share/regman
The registry manual's records, read as
.BR regman (1)
reads them, with its index at
.IR /var/cache/regman/index .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR reg (1),
.BR regman (1),
.BR gxwi-sd-editor (1),
.BR gxwi-file-dialog (1).
.PP
Registry Editor, at learn.peios.org.
